maint/spec-drift-check.pl compares the k8s() attribute registry against the
spec's `properties`. apiVersion and kind are not registry attributes -- they
come from IO::K8s::Role::APIObject as methods and are written by
Role::Resource::TO_JSON's `_is_resource` block. So the checker cannot see
whether a class claims a GVK it does not have, or lacks one it should.

Demonstrated by karr #45: seven shipped classes compose IO::K8s::APIObject
for types the spec gives no x-kubernetes-group-version-kind, and the run is
clean:

  Info  EXTRA FIELD  0   Perl declares a field the current spec no longer has

The fact needed to catch it is already in the spec and already read by the
script -- x-kubernetes-group-version-kind is what the Kind tiers key off.
It is simply not compared against the shipped class's TypeMeta behaviour.

WHAT TO ADD
A check, in whichever tier fits the script's existing severity model:

  for a shipped class whose definition key resolves in the spec:
    upstream has GVK, class does not emit TypeMeta  -> report
    upstream has no GVK, class emits TypeMeta       -> report

"emits TypeMeta" is `$class->can('_is_resource') && $class->_is_resource`,
which the script can ask without loading anything it does not already load
(it loads all 846 classes for the registry today).

Expected output against the tree as it stands: exactly the seven classes in
karr #45 and nothing else. That is the acceptance test -- verified by hand
in that ticket, the same scan, so a first implementation can be checked
against a known answer. Core::V1::PersistentVolumeClaim must NOT appear (it
carries inline TypeMeta upstream and is embedded in StatefulSetSpec
legitimately); if it does, the check is keying off "is embedded" rather than
"has a GVK upstream".

The 15 tier-2 classes (karr #44) resolve to no definition key at all in the
current spec and are already covered by the perl_only exceptions -- make
sure the new check does not re-report them from the other direction.

Once it is in and karr #45 is fixed, the exception file gets no entries from
this: a clean run is the goal, not a suppressed one.

Do not touch lib/. maint/ only, plus a spec-drift-exceptions.yaml note if a
deliberate keep-it-as-is case turns up.

Found reviewing karr #45.
